# Rust notebook to PDF and transparent PNG

## Scope and existing evidence

Compile pasted text or Markdown into measured physical notebook pages with
headings, accepted glyphs and image overlays, then export real PDF/PNG using
the existing Rust authority. Overflow must refuse without silent clipping.

The historical accepted theoretical design is preserved in completed P1–P9
records. This record is **open executable integration work**. It must not be
closed merely because a prior design was accepted or a different
implementation was proposed.

## Verified printed-date partial integration — 2026-11-03

The Rust notebook profile now admits an optional, validated numeric Gregorian
date. The same native paper compositor prints the selected date as fixed-width
vector marks in multi-page PDF and transparent PNG, and refuses impossible
dates or date text without a placeholder. Existing seven-field notebook v1
profiles reopen with an empty date and preserve their previous paper output.

Tests cover native serialization, CLI files, authenticated HTTP, and an actual
Firefox local save/reopen/CLI export. The Firefox test also preserves an exact
Spanish `ñ` statistical override, without claiming a rendered ink glyph.

**Still missing:** body handwriting strokes, complete Spanish/English glyph
shapes and fallbacks, embedded image overlays, and a single saved notebook
containing all content. The output remains a genuine paper layer, not full
notebook content or complete acceptance of P5-011.

## Caller-authored pen-segment overlay (partial) — 2026-11-03

Rust now accepts up to 512 explicit, user-authored physical straight pen
segments with one-based page identity, printable-region endpoint and
stroke-width clearance, RGB ink, and a refusal for invalid page/geometry.
The same native path serialization emits real page-specific vector PDF ink,
and a bounded transparent RGBA rasterizer emits diagonals at the selected
DPI without turning the entire segment bounding box opaque.

A two-page
A4 CLI smoke export (`atrament-profiles notebook --export-paper pdf --pages 2
--ink-line 2 25400 25400 50800 50800 700 '#DE1144'`) passed `pdfinfo`;
selected PNG page 2 has 596 × 842 RGBA pixels at 72 DPI. Test assertions
inspect the decoded PNG pixel channels, PDF page count/path/color, and
explicit refusal of oversize, invalid and off-sheet lines before an existing
file can change. No separate Python runtime or renderer was used.

**Scope:** These are manual caller-selected strokes, not rendered glyphs,
statistically synthesized handwriting or stored notebook content. Notebook
v1 JSON and authenticated localhost profile editor do not persist or expose
these segments. Image overlays, owned glyph shapes/fallbacks, and a saved
two-page content notebook remain unimplemented; this does **not** complete
the P5-011 acceptance criteria.

## Exact authored glyph ink overlay (partial) — 2026-11-03

A bounded UTF-8 text run can now select caller-drawn per-grapheme pen
segments on numbered pages with `--glyph-stroke` and `--place-text`.
The emitted vector PDF and selected-page transparent PNG share the existing
Rust ink path and refuse missing glyph shapes, oversized text/ink, invalid
page geometry or output replacement before writing. A new regression proves
that page-2 glyph ink equals individually specified page-2 pen segments in
both formats and is not silently copied to page 1.

The CLI additionally accepts a separately saved, validated
`atrament.handwriting.statistics.v1` profile for **mean-based** width, height,
ink-thickness, letter-spacing and baseline-offset transformations of the
explicit glyph segments. PDF and PNG share the resulting physical strokes;
unsupported statistical variables remain numeric-only, and output still
requires every visible grapheme to have a caller-authored shape.

There is no saved notebook body-text schema, visual glyph editor, complete
font/stroke set or image layer; these arguments are export-only. This is
not a completed P5-011 end-to-end notebook renderer.

## Opt-in marker-style Markdown heading decoration (partial) — 2026-11-03

The Rust `atrament-profiles notebook --flow-markdown` path can now select
`--heading-style underline|marker|none` and an optional validated
`--heading-color '#RRGGBB'`. Default behavior remains the existing navy
single-stroke underline.

The marker option uses four parallel, overlapping
1.4-mm **opaque** pastel strokes on the heading text baseline region,
drawn **before** the caller-authored glyph ink by the existing physical
PDF and transparent PNG compositor; it does not synthesize letterforms or
modify glyph templates. A custom color can also recolor the ordinary
underline. Bullet marks and page-specific dates/printed paper remain
independent of heading decoration.

Regression tests verify four colored strips, physical positions and stroke
order relative to glyph ink, original underline defaults, a no-decoration
choice that preserves bullets, repeatable genuine PDF/PNG output, malformed
or duplicate flag refusal, and an unchanged existing target file on every
refusal. A combined two-page Markdown test checks 36 accented authored
headings, **144 colored marker strokes**, per-occurrence seeded ink and
both PDF/PNG page-2 replay. The independent native CLI produced a genuine
two-page A4 PDF and 596×842 transparent RGBA page-2 PNG; direct pixel
inspection of an additional one-page PNG found 208 exact selected marker
pixels and 25 original authored-ink pixels against transparent background.

A separate bound regression admits 110 one-stroke default-underlined
headings but refuses the same 110 headings with a four-stroke marker before
replacing an existing file: 550 candidate strokes exceed the unchanged
512-stroke compositor budget. Custom heading flags require Markdown flow,
and all decorative segments count against printable bounds.

This is an **export-only** presentation choice, not a saved notebook style,
translucent marker simulation or browser text composition; neither images
nor a persisted two-page notebook document are implemented. P5-011 remains
open for its full native saved-content and image-layer acceptance.

## Round-capped authored PNG ink and border order — 2026-11-04

The native transparent PNG compositor now distinguishes the explicit ink
segments appended after historical paper marks. Horizontal, vertical and
diagonal authored pen strokes use rounded caps at physical pixel centers,
matching the existing vector PDF round-cap rule; paper-only and printed-mark
rectangles retain their previous rasterization and deterministic bytes.

When a rounded notebook border is selected, the PNG now paints it before
authored pen segments, matching the PDF's foreground ink precedence. Two
red-to-green 150-DPI Rust pixel regressions cover previously opaque square
corners and overlapping ink/border color, with transparent untouched pixels.
All 35 paper-library tests and 17 real browser/native PDF+PNG integration
tests passed. Image overlays and the saved complete notebook remain open.

## Acceptance decision

Open. Provide the actual bounded Rust/backend or browser integration and an
accepted/refused example before marking the executable capability complete.
Theoretical design acceptance alone cannot establish that this feature runs.

## Manual-only native PDF page inference (partial) — 2026-11-10

The Rust `atrament-profiles notebook` CLI now infers a manual-only PDF's
physical page count from the last explicit `--ink-line` or `--place-text`
page, matching the existing flowed-export behavior. A selected manual mark
on page 3 produces a three-page vector PDF without separately specifying
`--pages 3`. An explicit `--pages` remains an upper bound and refuses if
it would omit any manual page.

A native subprocess regression checks page-two authored glyphs and page-three
pen ink independently against selected transparent RGBA PNGs, plus exact
byte parity between automatically inferred and explicitly sized PDFs for
both manual mark families and their combination. It checks that an
insufficient page budget, missing later glyph shape and off-margin later
ink refuse without changing accepted PDF or PNG destinations.

This change does not persist notebook content, infer handwriting shapes,
introduce image overlays or expose browser-rendered body text. The
complete saved-notebook PDF/PNG acceptance remains open.

## Sparse final-sheet native PDF/PNG acceptance (partial) — 2026-11-10

A bounded native CLI regression now exercises the maximum 32 physical pages
with a manually positioned glyph run on page 32 and an independent pen segment
on page 31. It repeats this under standalone manual ink, one-page plain-text
flow and one-page Markdown flow in a fixed region. Automatically sized PDFs
contain exactly 32 pages and are byte-identical to explicitly sized output;
transparent PNG pages 31 and 32 match their respective manual-only sources.
The first flowed page remains unchanged when the late annotations are added.

An explicit 31-page PDF budget, unshaped glyph on an unselected later page,
invalid page-31 physical ink and out-of-range page 33 all refuse without
replacing accepted PDF or PNG files. Flowed output also refuses an empty
page-30 PNG selection. This guards sparse last-sheet behavior, not persisted
notebook text, object avoidance, visual editing or image overlays.

Validation: 13/13 native selected-file CLI tests, 2,189 all-feature Rust
workspace tests, offline workspace check, strict production Clippy, frontend
TypeScript, native flow shard 49 (3/3), print-ready (1/1), joined ink (3/3)
and Git diff checks passed. Jig check retains 181 known unrelated findings,
zero on changed paths. Jig doctor exits successfully but reports a degraded
state because its compiled graph is absent; do not claim a clean Jig gate.

## Landscape Letter mixed-layer physical replay (partial) — 2026-11-10

An additional real native CLI regression reopens a caller-saved landscape
US Letter paper profile (279.4 × 215.9 mm) and overlays one-page plain or
Markdown flow with pen ink on page 31 and explicitly placed authored glyphs
on page 32. Both modes emit exactly 32 vector PDF pages with 792 × 612 point
MediaBoxes, byte-identical to explicit `--pages 32`; selected 72-DPI
transparent PNGs are 792 × 612 RGBA. Their late-page bytes equal independent
manual-only exports with the same printed date and page numbering. The first
flow page remains byte-identical with or without late annotations, and empty
numbered sheets 31 and 32 remain distinguishable.

The regression also requires that an insufficient explicit PDF page budget,
page-32 ink outside landscape margins and nonexistent page 33 refuse before
replacing accepted PDF/PNG destinations. The selected paper file remains
byte-identical. This extends the previously A4-only sparse-last-page
acceptance to a second physical page geometry; it does not persist body text,
provide image overlays or complete the saved-notebook journey.

Validation: 14/14 native selected-file CLI cases, 2,189 offline all-feature
Rust workspace tests, workspace check, strict pinned production Clippy,
frontend TypeScript, native flow shard 49 (3/3), print-ready (1/1) and joined
ink (3/3) passed. Jig doctor exits successfully in degraded state (compiled
graph absent); Jig check still reports 181 existing findings, none on these
two changed paths. The full native-flow
shard suite was not replayed for this test-only addition.

## Explicit saved physical ink input (partial) — 2026-11-10

The native Rust notebook CLI can now read a caller-selected, versioned
`atrament.page-ink.v1` UTF-8 TSV through `--ink-file`. Every tab-separated
row provides page, physical endpoints, pen width and RGB color; it reuses
exact `--ink-line` validation and the existing PDF/PNG compositor. A 256-KiB
file cap and combined 4096-segment bound apply. The same safe regular-file
reader refuses symlinks, parent traversal, oversize or invalid UTF-8 input.

Two native subprocess tests compare exact PDF and transparent PNG bytes of
saved ink against identical inline segments on pages 1, 31 and 32, with and
without plain/Markdown flow. They check automatic PDF page inference, the
explicit page budget, combining multiple ink files and unchanged input files.
Invalid schema, blank/malformed rows, bad colors/pages, invalid UTF-8,
oversized files, excess segments and input symlinks all refuse without
overwriting previously accepted PDF or PNG output.

This adds reusable manually positioned ink as a separate caller-owned file.
It does **not** save notebook body text, embed images, synthesize missing
glyphs or expose the flow source and ink in the localhost editor. The
complete saved-notebook PDF/PNG acceptance remains open.
